[Music]  March of 2017 was a bipolar time for  video games early in the month Nintendo  released the Legend of Zelda breath of a  while to critical acclaim and commercial  success it set a new bar for open-world  titles and propelled the Nintendo switch  to a promising launch on March 21st  however Bioware released Mass Effect  Andromeda it was buggy it was poorly  paced it was a troubling start for a new  chapter in the iconic sci-fi series and  even now after numerous patches and  updates it's still a weak game overall  these two games have something in common  though they were both delayed on several  occasions for various reasons the  studio's behind Zelda and Mass Effect  felt they needed breathing room and  changed their release dates and despite  their differences these projects  illustrate the benefits of delays one  title released when it was finally ready  and the other clearly could have used  more time recently developers rock star  and monolith Studios announced delays  for Red Dead Redemption 2 and Middle  Earth shadow of war respectively and  while this is initially disappointing  for people anticipating those games now  is as good a time as any to take a  closer look at delays to examine how  hard they are to actually pull off and  to discuss why we as players should be  relieved when they happen  [Music]  the video game industry is not what it  used to be  publishers offer deluxe editions season  passes and pre-order bonuses for what  seems like every major game release  we're asked to buy a title months  sometimes a year before we'll ever get  to play it maybe it's because of this  relationship that a stigma has long been  attached to game delays we pay upfront  for a game and all of its future DLC  expecting it on a certain date sometimes  planning other game purchases around it  so when the developer needs more time to  finish the game it can be mistaken for a  broken promise we kept our end of the  bargain so why didn't they this consumer  creator relationship becomes dicey or  when you consider Kickstarter campaigns  rather than release the finished product  to an expectant audience  Kickstarter allows developers to ask for  money up front in turn allowing the  player to be involved in the funding  process itself many creators provide  updates to patrons along the way as  behind the scene rewards or even just to  ensure them that work is being done to  make good on their investment but in  this relationship the creator doesn't  have the same accountability it might in  the traditional sales model here or a  developer can take as much time as it  wants in the creation process promising  a vague release date that could change  at any given moment to be clear I don't  have any inherent problems with this  model some of my favorite games in  recent years began as Kickstarter  campaigns from darkest dungeon to hyper  light drifter to the Banner Saga and of  course FTL but when kickstarter  purgatory grows larger by the year  people begin to notice that their  investments are not coming to fruition  as a February video game project took in  60% less revenue than they did the year  before through Kickstarter of course  we're also in the middle of an early  access craze in theory this is a  fascinating funding model we pay an  arbitrary amount for what we acknowledge  to be a playable but unfinished game  along the way we'll see patches  developer updates and probably added  features ten minutes before writing this  episode I played player unknowns  battlegrounds and dead cells both of  which are in early access on Steam and  both of which are fantastic games  already but like Kickstarter games the  early access model has its own  complications despite the fact that many  games in this category are playable and  missing only a few last minute details  there are others that are nowhere near  complete and have no solid release date  despite being in early  says for much longer than they promised  people who by Enza these games also  assume the risk of these products being  inferior  [Music]  when Mass Effect Andromeda released it  was riddled with bugs clunky animations  broken quest lines janky controls and  myriad glitches soon after Bioware  Montreal released a statement saying  that it would fix Andromeda over time  yes a Mass Effect game released at full  price and its creators said it would  improve it as it goes now I understand  that games aren't what they used to be  many of them serve more as platforms  than traditional games destiny and  overwatch come to mind but when an RPG  developer releases a buggy triple-a game  knowing full well that it will have to  fix not support fix a game after launch  shows major flaws in the system people  paid $60 maybe more in some cases to  play Mass Effect on March 21st and  instead got a buggy mess that would only  gradually become the Mass Effect they  expected  [Music]  I don't mean to keep kicking Mass Effect  while it's down I know the studio  underwent numerous personnel changes and  management problems throughout the  course of Andromeda's development I  don't know the full story but I know it  wasn't the ideal environment for a team  to make a quality game and I know how  hard it is to actually delay a game  Andromeda itself was delayed several  times and during that process I was  happy the internal studio problems were  a clear indicator that the developer  needed more time and I'm sure a lot of  thought and pivoting went into those  delays marketing campaigns are planned  months sometimes years in advance for  Triple A games much of any given  campaign is structured around a specific  release date and changing that date can  throw a whole campaign off furthermore  much of triple-a development is  interwoven between studios members that  work on one title may be scheduled to  work on another as soon as their current  project releases triple-a development is  also very much a cascading structure  where the money made from one game  largely goes into making the next the  delay a game too much could cost  personnel and funding for the next  project the team wants to pursue all of  this is to say that game delays are in  no way a simple strategy for a developer  or publisher they cost money time and  resources so for a developer to take a  closer look at the project they've spent  years on and tell us that they need more  time to realize their vision should be a  great thing I'm not upset about shadow  of war getting delayed or a Red Dead  Redemption - getting pushback because  I'm looking forward to hopefully playing  these games in a finished state when  they do release  [Music]  you've likely played at least one if not  most of these games and for good reason  they're all fantastic some of them are  close to perfect and yet they were all  at one point or another delayed the  cause of those delays range from famous  to obscure the story of half-life 2's  development for instance has become the  stuff of legend years of broken promises  legal battles and scheduling problems  led up to the final release reasons for  breath of the wilds delay on the other  hand are a bit more opaque Nintendo just  didn't think the game was ready and so  it held on to it but of course we don't  remember all of these games because of  their delays we remember them for what  they are now they're classics there look  - as examples of how amazing games can  be their Testaments to the power of  games as a creative medium over their  value as a business industry I think  there needs to be more trust between  creators and consumers between  developers and the fans playing their  games I think we need to understand that  when Rockstar delays read that  redemption - or monolith delays Middle  Earth shadow of war that is a difficult  choice and most likely not a bad thing  for us at all in fact it's largely the  opposite it's - creators saying their  work isn't ready for the people who want  to play them it's - developers saying  yes video games are a business but  they're also a creative medium in art  form and we want to release the best  possible version of this project we're  pouring our lives into we want our time  to be worth it and we want to make your  time worth it too  [Music]  hey everyone thanks so much for watching  episode 8 of reboot this was actually  the last episode of season 1 we're  taking the week off next week to go down  to Los Angeles for e3 but in a couple  weeks when we come back we will start  season 2 so as always get your questions  and comments in but this time tell us  not just what you liked or didn't like  about episode 8 tell us what you like  about the show anything you could  comment on critique we'll talk about  that and our next episode we'll see you  then

